{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And He said.</b> After it says \"Moses returned,\" what reason is there to say \"Moses reported the people's words to YHWH\"? Know that there are many verses in the Torah that ought to have come earlier, and their meaning is: it had already been thus and so — as in <b>וייצר יהוה אלהים</b> (Genesis 2:7), meaning \"and [He] had already formed\"; <b>ויצמח יהוה אלהים</b> (ibid. v. 9), \"and [He] had already caused to grow\"; <b>ויאמר בת מי את</b> (ibid. 24:23), \"and [he] had already said.\" Likewise it is written \"I asked her and said,\" and afterward \"I put the ring on her nose\" (ibid. vv. 47ff.), and <b>ותאמר אל העבד</b> (ibid. v. 65), \"and she had already said to the servant\"; <b>ותפול מעל הגמל</b> (ibid. v. 64) — there is no need to elaborate. So too here: \"Moses reported\" — he had already reported, but Scripture did not mention what he reported. Now the word הגיד always refers to something new that was not previously mentioned, so it does not connect with \"Moses returned.\" From HaShem's response, in which He said \"Behold, I come to you in the thickness of the cloud,\" we learn what he reported. And furthermore we find this matter stated explicitly in Moses' words, as I shall explain. Know that Egypt and India are of the descendants of Ham, and these rely on those. The people of India do not eat meat, and so too the Egyptians used to do, and they did not depart from this custom except because the kingdom of Ishmael overpowered them and they reverted to its religion. And the sages of India bring proofs according to their reasoning, namely that it is impossible that HaShem should speak with a man and he live. Israel were in Egypt, and there were among them men who held to this belief, and the prophecy of Moses was in doubt for them. And there is no argument from the verse \"they believed in YHWH and in Moses His servant\" (above, 14:31), for it is written \"Israel saw\" — not \"all Israel.\" And even if it had been written \"all Israel,\" it would refer to the majority according to the rule of language, as in <b>וימת כל מקנה מצרים</b> (above 9:6), and afterward it is written: \"send your livestock\" (ibid. v. 19)." "Now the meaning of \"I come to you\" is a response to Moses' words, which he had thus reported to Him." "<b>And the meaning of \"in the thickness of the cloud.\"</b> When HaShem descended upon the mountain." "<b>And \"with your words.\"</b> The Ten Commandments. Then they will believe, for it is indeed possible that HaShem speaks with a man and he lives." "<b>And the meaning of \"and they will also believe in you.\"</b> That you are a prophet, and the doubt will be removed from their thoughts. For it is explicitly written: \"This day we have seen that Elohim speaks with man and he lives\" (Deuteronomy 5:21), and furthermore it is written there: \"For who of all flesh has heard the voice of living Elohim speaking from the midst of the fire, as we have, and lived?\" (ibid. v. 23), and there it is written: \"You approach and hear\" (ibid. v. 24), and this is \"and they will also believe in you forever.\""], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:36:03.130646409Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}